All three questions land. You're right — Approach 1 as I wrote it is spiritually what we want but mechanically wasteful. The classifier *shape* is identical across all 5 agents (run subprocess → map stdout/stderr/exit/execErr → `TextGenError`); only the *data* differs per CLI. Let me refine to Approach 1b — shared engine + per-agent data.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
The generic skeleton every agent needs is: `exec.ErrNotFound → cli_missing`, ctx cancel → passthrough sentinel, exit code + stderr → phrase-match → Kind. That code is identical in all 5 agents. What changes per CLI: (a) the phrase table, (b) the remediation copy, (c) Claude *additionally* has a JSON-envelope path nothing else has. So: factor the engine into `agent/`, keep per-agent packages to a tiny declarative config.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

---

### Approach 1b — Shared engine, per-agent declarative config (recommended)

**Shared in `cmd/entire/cli/agent/text_gen_error.go`:**

```go
type TextGenErrorKind string  // auth | rate_limit | config | cli_missing | unknown

type TextGenError struct {
    Kind      TextGenErrorKind
    Provider  types.AgentName  // drives remediation copy
    Message   string
    APIStatus int              // only populated when a CLI gives us HTTP status (Claude)
    ExitCode  int
    Cause     error
}

// PhraseRule: a substring → Kind mapping. Evaluated case-insensitive, first-match-wins.
type PhraseRule struct { Kind TextGenErrorKind; Phrase string }

// Classifier is agent-provided declarative data; the engine below consumes it.
type Classifier struct {
    Provider      types.AgentName
    Phrases       []PhraseRule           // per-CLI, ordered
    ParseEnvelope EnvelopeParser         // optional — only Claude uses this
}

// Execution helper runs the CLI and returns raw signals — no pre-formatting.
type ExecResult struct { Stdout, Stderr []byte; ExitCode int }
func RunIsolatedTextGeneratorCLIRaw(...) (ExecResult, error)

// Single entry point all agents use:
func (c *Classifier) Classify(res ExecResult, runErr error) *TextGenError { ... }
```

**Per-agent** — each `generate.go` declares its classifier data and calls one helper:

```go
// codex/generate.go
var codexClassifier = &agent.Classifier{
    Provider: agent.AgentNameCodex,
    Phrases: []agent.PhraseRule{
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorAuth, Phrase: "OPENAI_API_KEY"},
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorRateLimit, Phrase: "rate limit"},
        // …data-only, seeded from the research pass
    },
}
func (c *CodexAgent) GenerateText(ctx context.Context, prompt, model string) (string, error) {
    res, err := agent.RunIsolatedTextGeneratorCLIRaw(ctx, c.CommandRunner, "codex", args, prompt)
    if classErr := codexClassifier.Classify(res, err); classErr != nil { return "", classErr }
    return strings.TrimSpace(string(res.Stdout)), nil
}
```

**Claude** gets the envelope parser plugged in:
```go
var claudeClassifier = &agent.Classifier{
    Provider:      agent.AgentNameClaudeCode,
    Phrases:       authStderrRules,        // small fallback — same as 963
    ParseEnvelope: claudeEnvelopeParser,   // JSON envelope + api_error_status
}
```
`claudecode/generate.go` shrinks — no more inline case logic.

**HTTP-status baseline** — the engine matches `"401"` / `"403"` / `"429"` automatically before per-agent phrases, because those substrings show up in every CLI's passthrough of API errors. One place, not five.

**Remediation in `explain.go`** — single switch on `Kind`, remediation looked up from one table:
```go
var remediation = map[types.AgentName]map[TextGenErrorKind]string{
    ClaudeCode: {Auth: "Run `claude login` and retry", …},
    Codex:      {Auth: "Run `codex login` and retry", …},
    // …
}
```
`formatCheckpointSummaryError` becomes one switch. No 5-way `errors.As`.

---

### Answering your three questions against Approach 1b

**1. Extensible?** Adding a 6th agent = declare one `Classifier{}` literal + one row in the remediation table. No new file matching `error.go`, no touching `explain.go`'s control flow, no new `errors.As` branch. That's as extensible as it gets.

**2. Matches the codebase?** Yes — this is the same "engine in `agent/`, per-agent config in agent package" shape as `RunIsolatedTextGeneratorCLI` + `summaryProviderBinaries` today. Compare to `text_generator_cli.go:73-79`: one shared helper, one small map declaring per-agent data. Same pattern.

**3. Duplication?** One copy of the classification loop, one copy of `TextGenError` construction, one copy of `cli_missing` detection, one copy of ctx sentinels. Per-agent footprint is a literal struct of data plus one-line call into `Classify`.

---

**Do you want Approach 1b? And same Q4: can I do the CLI research pass myself, or do you have captured outputs from the 887 manual test pass I should use?**

