content.text:
`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
Three of four non-Claude CLIs exit 1 on auth failure; Gemini exits **41** (node.js exit-code convention, idiosyncratic). So exit code alone is a poor classifier — stderr substring wins. Also: Codex's stderr carries literal `"401 Unauthorized"` because it's actually hitting the OpenAI API over websocket; Cursor/Copilot/Gemini reject client-side before any HTTP call, so no HTTP status appears in their stderr. This confirms we can't have a single cross-CLI classifier — per-CLI phrase data is load-bearing.

Also worth noting: each CLI's own error message **already contains** the remediation guidance. Cursor literally says `"Please run 'agent login' first, or set CURSOR_API_KEY"`. Copilot enumerates three auth paths. We have a design choice: synthesize our own remediation copy, or preserve the CLI's own message verbatim (so users get single-source-of-truth from the CLI they actually installed).
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

## Research findings — actual stderr shapes per CLI

| CLI | Exit | Auth-Kind phrases (confirmed) | Remediation built into stderr |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Claude Code** | 0 (envelope) | `api_error_status: 401/403`; fallback `"invalid api key"`, `"not logged in"` | no — we synthesize "Run `claude login`" |
| **Codex** | 1 | `"401 Unauthorized"`, `"Missing bearer or basic authentication"` | no explicit remediation |
| **Gemini** | **41** | `"Please set an Auth method"`, `"GEMINI_API_KEY"` | "set GEMINI_API_KEY / GOOGLE_GENAI_USE_VERTEXAI / GOOGLE_GENAI_USE_GCA" |
| **Cursor** | 1 | `"Authentication required"`, `"CURSOR_API_KEY"` | "Run `agent login` or set CURSOR_API_KEY" |
| **Copilot** | 1 | `"No authentication information found"`, `"COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN"` | "Run `copilot /login` or `gh auth login` or set COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN/GH_TOKEN/GITHUB_TOKEN" |

**Not verified empirically** (seeded from common HTTP conventions, flagged in spec for follow-up refinement):
- `rate_limit`: substrings `"rate limit"`, `"quota"`, `"429"` — need a live authenticated call hitting quota to verify.
- `config` (invalid model etc.): substrings `"model not found"`, `"invalid model"`, `"400"`, `"404"` — all four CLIs tripped the auth gate before model validation in my tests, so bad-model classifier shapes can only be seeded conservatively until a user reports one.

**CLI-missing** (`exec.ErrNotFound`) — identical across all 5 providers, engine handles it once.

---

## Design

I'll present this in sections. Approve each one (or push back) before I write it up formally.

### § 1. Shared types and engine — `cmd/entire/cli/agent/text_gen_error.go`

```go
// TextGenErrorKind classifies a text-generation subprocess failure.
type TextGenErrorKind string
const (
    TextGenErrorAuth       TextGenErrorKind = "auth"
    TextGenErrorRateLimit  TextGenErrorKind = "rate_limit"
    TextGenErrorConfig     TextGenErrorKind = "config"
    TextGenErrorCLIMissing TextGenErrorKind = "cli_missing"
    TextGenErrorUnknown    TextGenErrorKind = "unknown"
)

// TextGenError is the shared typed error every summary provider returns.
// APIStatus and ExitCode use zero for "not applicable".
type TextGenError struct {
    Kind      TextGenErrorKind
    Provider  types.AgentName
    Message   string         // user-safe text from the CLI (stderr excerpt or envelope result)
    APIStatus int            // only Claude's envelope populates this
    ExitCode  int
    Cause     error
}
func (e *TextGenError) Error() string { /* "provider CLI error (kind=...): message" */ }
func (e *TextGenError) Unwrap() error { return e.Cause }

// PhraseRule: substring→Kind, case-insensitive, first-match-wins.
type PhraseRule struct { Kind TextGenErrorKind; Phrase string }

// EnvelopeResult is what an envelope parser returns when it recognizes an error.
type EnvelopeResult struct { Kind TextGenErrorKind; Message string; APIStatus int }

// Classifier is declarative per-agent config consumed by the shared engine.
type Classifier struct {
    Provider      types.AgentName
    Phrases       []PhraseRule                                 // per-CLI, ordered
    ParseEnvelope func(stdout []byte) (*EnvelopeResult, bool)  // optional — Claude only
}

// ExecResult is what RunIsolatedTextGeneratorCLIRaw returns.
type ExecResult struct { Stdout, Stderr []byte; ExitCode int }

// Classify converts raw subprocess signals into *TextGenError. Nil-result means success.
// Logic: ctx sentinels passthrough; exec.ErrNotFound → CLIMissing; envelope parser (if set);
// HTTP-status baseline substrings; per-agent phrases; else Unknown.
func (c *Classifier) Classify(res ExecResult, runErr error) error { ... }

// RunIsolatedTextGeneratorCLIRaw replaces RunIsolatedTextGeneratorCLI for the
// summary path. Returns raw pieces instead of a formatted string.
func RunIsolatedTextGeneratorCLIRaw(ctx context.Context, runner TextCommandRunner,
    binary string, args []string, stdin string) (ExecResult, error)
```

The baseline HTTP-status phrases (`"401"`, `"403"`, `"429"`) live in the engine, checked **after** the envelope parser but **before** per-agent phrases, so Codex's `"401 Unauthorized"` is caught uniformly.

### § 2. Per-agent classifiers — declarative data only

**`codex/error.go`:**
```go
var classifier = &agent.Classifier{
    Provider: agent.AgentNameCodex,
    Phrases: []agent.PhraseRule{
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorAuth, Phrase: "Missing bearer or basic authentication"},
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorRateLimit, Phrase: "rate limit"},
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorRateLimit, Phrase: "quota"},
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorConfig, Phrase: "model not found"},
    },
}
```

**`geminicli/error.go`:**
```go
var classifier = &agent.Classifier{
    Provider: agent.AgentNameGemini,
    Phrases: []agent.PhraseRule{
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorAuth, Phrase: "Please set an Auth method"},
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorAuth, Phrase: "GEMINI_API_KEY"},
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorRateLimit, Phrase: "rate limit"},
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorRateLimit, Phrase: "quota"},
    },
}
```

**`cursor/error.go`:**
```go
var classifier = &agent.Classifier{
    Provider: agent.AgentNameCursor,
    Phrases: []agent.PhraseRule{
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorAuth, Phrase: "Authentication required"},
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorAuth, Phrase: "CURSOR_API_KEY"},
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorRateLimit, Phrase: "rate limit"},
    },
}
```

**`copilotcli/error.go`:**
```go
var classifier = &agent.Classifier{
    Provider: agent.AgentNameCopilotCLI,
    Phrases: []agent.PhraseRule{
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorAuth, Phrase: "No authentication information found"},
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorAuth, Phrase: "COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN"},
        {Kind: agent.TextGenErrorRateLimit, Phrase: "rate limit"},
    },
}
```

**`claudecode/error.go`:** unchanged logic, but migrated to emit `*agent.TextGenError`. Envelope parser (HTTP status → Kind) moves to `ParseEnvelope` hook. `ClaudeError` type deleted; its 3 call sites (`explain.go`, `explain_test.go`, `summarize_test.go`) migrate to `*agent.TextGenError`.

### § 3. Remediation mapping — single table in `explain.go`

```go
// Keyed by provider → kind → user-facing copy. Empty string falls back to a generic message.
var remediation = map[types.AgentName]map[agent.TextGenErrorKind]string{
    agent.AgentNameClaudeCode: {
        agent.TextGenErrorAuth:       "Run `claude login` and retry",
        agent.TextGenErrorRateLimit:  "Wait and retry",
        agent.TextGenErrorConfig:     "Check your Claude CLI config and selected model",
    },
    agent.AgentNameCodex: {
        agent.TextGenErrorAuth:       "Run `codex login` or set OPENAI_API_KEY",
        agent.TextGenErrorRateLimit:  "Wait and retry",
        agent.TextGenErrorConfig:     "Check your Codex CLI config and selected model",
    },
    agent.AgentNameGemini: {
        agent.TextGenErrorAuth:       "Set GEMINI_API_KEY, or configure auth in ~/.gemini/settings.json",
        agent.TextGenErrorRateLimit:  "Wait and retry",
        agent.TextGenErrorConfig:     "Check your Gemini CLI config and selected model",
    },
    agent.AgentNameCursor: {
        agent.TextGenErrorAuth:       "Run `agent login` or set CURSOR_API_KEY",
        agent.TextGenErrorRateLimit:  "Wait and retry",
        agent.TextGenErrorConfig:     "Check your Cursor agent config and selected model",
    },
    agent.AgentNameCopilotCLI: {
        agent.TextGenErrorAuth:       "Run `gh auth login`, `copilot /login`, or set COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN",
        agent.TextGenErrorRateLimit:  "Wait and retry",
        agent.TextGenErrorConfig:     "Check your Copilot CLI config and selected model",
    },
}
```

`formatCheckpointSummaryError` becomes a single switch on `*agent.TextGenError.Kind` using `e.Provider` to look up remediation. The `cli_missing` branch is provider-agnostic (`"<Display> CLI is not installed or not on PATH"`). The unknown/default branch preserves the existing "never empty" fallback logic (message > APIStatus > ExitCode).

### § 4. Testing

- `agent/text_gen_error_test.go`: engine tests — exec.ErrNotFound→CLIMissing, ctx cancel/deadline passthrough, HTTP-status baseline, per-agent phrase precedence after envelope, never-empty output.
- Per-agent `error_test.go`: phrase-match table test, using the **captured real stderr strings from this research pass** as fixtures. (That makes the test tied to actual CLI output, and a diff in those fixtures signals we need to re-run the research pass.)
- `claudecode/error_test.go` existing tests migrate to assert `*agent.TextGenError`.
- `explain_test.go`: one table test per provider × Kind asserting the final user-facing message.

### § 5. Migration / cleanup

- Delete `claudecode.ClaudeError` (no external callers).
- `RunIsolatedTextGeneratorCLI` (string-error version) deleted — all 4 call sites migrated to `RunIsolatedTextGeneratorCLIRaw`. Tests for the old helper migrate 1:1.
- Claude's direct-subprocess logic in `claudecode/generate.go` collapses back into the shared `RunIsolatedTextGeneratorCLIRaw` helper now that raw pieces are accessible; the envelope parser moves to `ParseEnvelope`.

---

## Two decisions I need from you before writing the spec

**D1. Remediation copy: synthesize ours, or preserve CLI's own stderr?** Three approaches are viable. (i) Synthesize — short, consistent across providers, what I drafted above. (ii) Preserve — wrap the CLI's own stderr message in our "Authentication failed: <stderr>" shell. More accurate (the CLI knows its own auth paths best) but longer and less consistent. (iii) Hybrid — synthesize short one-liner, append "(CLI said: <stderr excerpt>)" when available. **My pick: (i).** Matches 963's style and keeps messages scannable.

**D2. Do we migrate `claudecode.ClaudeError` fully, or keep it as a thin alias?** Fully migrating means deleting the type and updating 3 call sites in this repo. The alternative is keeping `ClaudeError` as a wrapper that embeds `*TextGenError`. **My pick: full migration.** No external callers, no deprecation risk, cleaner final state.

Tell me D1 and D2, or push back on any section, and I'll write the spec.

